Script

(introduction)

For this project, we have investigated the Faber-Jackson relation using data from the SAMI Galaxy Survey. 
The SAMI galaxy survey uses 3D spectroscopy to study how galaxies form and evolve and the data collected can be used to plot the Faber-Jackson relation which is a projection of the fundamental plane for early type galaxies. 
This relation traditionally links luminosity to the velocity dispersion. However, absolute magnitude can be related by the equation M_r = -2.5 log(L) + constant and can therefore allow absolute magnitude to be plotted instead. 
As a result, this relation is expected to show brighter and more massive galaxies with stars moving with a larger spread of velocities. 
Astronomers can measure the spectral velocity dispersion to estimate the absolute luminosity and compare with apparent magnitude to calculated its distance. 

With this research, we were then able to sort the data appropriately for the Faber Jackson relation. 
The first step was understanding what each file contained and what each variable represented. 
We were able to understand that the data was split across the four files but contained matching IDs. 
Therefore, each file gave different data for each galaxy and required us to isolate and combine the appropriate variables. 
Initially we sorted the data by type since the Faber Jackson plot is used for Early Type galaxies. 
Using the data given by the visual morphology, we isolated the galaxies of type 0 to 1.5 which represent the elliptical and lenticular galaxies. 
We then isolated the IDs and type for each of the galaxies before finding their velocity dispersion sigma_re, the stellar mass m_star and the absolute magnitude m_r within the other files. 
However, to combine the files, we had to remove the duplicated IDs in certain files and remove data points containing NaN. 
